1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,306
Time to build the actual instrument, on your own account, using the batch that's

2
00:00:05,306 --> 00:00:08,775
already cleared every pre-flight check from Section six.

3
00:00:08,775 --> 00:00:12,058
Pull up your angle map and your cleared creative set.

4
00:00:12,058 --> 00:00:16,704
Every angle that survived pre-flight gets its own isolated ad set, the same

5
00:00:16,704 --> 00:00:18,748
structure Section 7.1 argued for.

6
00:00:18,748 --> 00:00:22,589
No mixing, no shared ad sets, one variable changing at a time.

7
00:00:22,589 --> 00:00:24,695
Notice what you're not doing here.

8
00:00:24,695 --> 00:00:29,775
You're not deciding whether an angle is any good, that question is Section eight's

9
00:00:29,775 --> 00:00:32,252
job, once real data exists to answer it.

10
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:37,394
You're only deciding how many ad sets you need and what each one is allowed to cost

11
00:00:37,394 --> 00:00:39,252
you before you'd call it dead.

12
00:00:39,252 --> 00:00:44,394
Keep those two questions separate, the same discipline Section 7.4 already asked of

13
00:00:44,394 --> 00:00:44,642
you.

14
00:00:44,642 --> 00:00:49,288
Ask yourself, before you build a single ad set: which of these angles would

15
00:00:49,288 --> 00:00:51,394
genuinely surprise you if it lost?

16
00:00:51,394 --> 00:00:54,181
If none of them would, that's worth noticing.

17
00:00:54,181 --> 00:00:59,323
A batch built entirely from angles you already feel confident about isn't much of a

18
00:00:59,323 --> 00:00:59,633
test.

19
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:04,650
The angles worth including are the ones you're genuinely unsure of, because those

20
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:08,925
are the only ones this architecture can teach you anything new about.

21
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:14,379
This is the whole worksheet, five lines, and every line traces back to a specific

22
00:01:14,379 --> 00:01:17,286
lecture you've already sat through this section.

23
00:01:17,286 --> 00:01:22,130
Your CAC ceiling comes from Section 1.5's maths, redone for your own margin, not

24
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:23,523
borrowed from Harlan's.

25
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:25,460
Fill in the two blanks honestly.

26
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:30,425
If you don't know your own contribution margin off the top of your head, stop here

27
00:01:30,425 --> 00:01:33,937
and work it out properly before you fill in anything else.

28
00:01:33,937 --> 00:01:38,963
A testing architecture built on a guessed margin is exactly the "precision borrowed

29
00:01:38,963 --> 00:01:43,262
from someone else's numbers" trap Section 7.2 already warned you about.

30
00:01:43,262 --> 00:01:46,411
Line five is worth a second look before you move on.

31
00:01:46,411 --> 00:01:51,315
"About two days' spend" only means something once you've filled in your own daily

32
00:01:51,315 --> 00:01:52,648
budget from line four.

33
00:01:52,648 --> 00:01:57,431
Do these in order, top to bottom, or a worksheet like this quietly ends up with

34
00:01:57,431 --> 00:01:58,945
numbers that don't agree.

35
00:01:58,945 --> 00:02:04,031
Notice, too, that line five doesn't hardcode zero add-to-carts the way Harlan's did.

36
00:02:04,031 --> 00:02:09,057
Your key signal might be something else, a lead form, a demo booking, whatever sits

37
00:02:09,057 --> 00:02:11,782
closest to a real purchase for what you sell.

38
00:02:11,782 --> 00:02:16,565
Pick that signal deliberately, the reasoning Section 7.2 walked through for why

39
00:02:16,565 --> 00:02:19,351
add-to-cart was right for Harlan specifically.

40
00:02:19,901 --> 00:02:24,753
About forty minutes, and notice more of it goes to the margin calculation than to

41
00:02:24,753 --> 00:02:26,970
actually configuring the ad platform.

42
00:02:26,970 --> 00:02:28,228
That's not backwards.

43
00:02:28,228 --> 00:02:32,901
Getting the number right matters more than clicking through the setup screens,

44
00:02:32,901 --> 00:02:37,154
because a wrong number makes every ad set built on top of it wrong too.

45
00:02:37,154 --> 00:02:42,126
If you're not ready to spend real money yet, build the structure anyway and hold it

46
00:02:42,126 --> 00:02:42,486
ready.

47
00:02:42,486 --> 00:02:46,619
A testing architecture sitting finished and unused costs you nothing.

48
00:02:46,619 --> 00:02:51,292
One built in a rush, the week you finally decide to spend, costs you the exact

49
00:02:51,292 --> 00:02:53,988
mistakes this whole section tried to prevent.

50
00:02:53,988 --> 00:02:58,960
Notice, too, that this table's time estimate assumes your margin calculation is the

51
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:00,877
one genuinely new piece of work.

52
00:03:00,877 --> 00:03:05,730
Everything else, the ad sets, the budgets, the kill rule, is mostly transcription

53
00:03:05,730 --> 00:03:07,647
once that first number is right.

54
00:03:07,647 --> 00:03:12,379
Rush the margin step to save ten minutes and you risk redoing the other thirty.

55
00:03:12,379 --> 00:03:17,112
Forty minutes is also a useful number to hold onto for a different reason: it's

56
00:03:17,112 --> 00:03:22,144
roughly what this whole architecture costs you to build, once, compared to the hours

57
00:03:22,144 --> 00:03:26,877
a badly structured account can waste over a single week of confused, unreadable

58
00:03:26,877 --> 00:03:27,356
results.

59
00:03:27,356 --> 00:03:32,209
That trade is the entire argument this section has been making since Section 7.1.

60
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:35,866
One honest expectation before you call this finished.

61
00:03:35,866 --> 00:03:40,438
If your CAC ceiling, your daily budget, and your kill rule all look completely

62
00:03:40,438 --> 00:03:45,245
different from Harlan's, that's the workshop working correctly, not a sign you did

63
00:03:45,245 --> 00:03:46,183
something wrong.

64
00:03:46,183 --> 00:03:48,762
Different margins produce different numbers.

65
00:03:48,762 --> 00:03:53,510
What matters is that every one of yours traces back to your own actual economics,

66
00:03:53,510 --> 00:03:55,973
the same way Harlan's traced back to hers.

67
00:03:55,973 --> 00:04:00,779
If your numbers happen to land close to Harlan's, don't treat that as confirmation

68
00:04:00,779 --> 00:04:02,245
you did it right, either.

69
00:04:02,245 --> 00:04:03,945
Check the actual maths again.

70
00:04:03,945 --> 00:04:08,752
A coincidental match is still a coincidence, not evidence, and this worksheet only

71
00:04:08,752 --> 00:04:13,617
means something if every figure on it can be traced back to your own margin, not to

72
00:04:13,617 --> 00:04:15,962
a number that happened to look familiar.

73
00:04:15,962 --> 00:04:19,831
The same honesty applies to the key signal you chose on line five.

74
00:04:19,831 --> 00:04:24,697
If you picked add-to-cart purely because that's what Harlan's example used, go back

75
00:04:24,697 --> 00:04:28,859
and ask whether it's actually the right signal for what you're selling.

76
00:04:28,859 --> 00:04:33,666
A subscription product, a service booking, a high-ticket item: each of those has a

77
00:04:33,666 --> 00:04:38,414
different behaviour that sits closest to real intent, and copying Harlan's choice

78
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:42,869
without checking is the same shortcut this whole callout is warning against.

79
00:04:43,419 --> 00:04:45,401
One lecture left in this section.

80
00:04:45,401 --> 00:04:50,327
Then Section eight takes whatever this architecture actually produces and reads it

81
00:04:50,327 --> 00:04:55,192
honestly: winner, loser, or noise, using exactly the discipline this section just

82
00:04:55,192 --> 00:04:55,552
built.

83
00:04:55,552 --> 00:05:00,537
Before that, finish the worksheet if you haven't already, even if you're weeks away

84
00:05:00,537 --> 00:05:02,579
from actually spending against it.

85
00:05:02,579 --> 00:05:07,264
Every lecture between here and the end of the course assumes this architecture

86
00:05:07,264 --> 00:05:12,069
exists, built on your own numbers, ready to use the moment you actually need it.

87
00:05:12,069 --> 00:05:16,994
Keep a copy somewhere you'll actually find it again, the same brief folder Section

88
00:05:16,994 --> 00:05:19,998
4.1 already asked you to build the habit of using.

89
00:05:19,998 --> 00:05:24,682
A testing architecture you can't locate six weeks from now is worth exactly as

90
00:05:24,682 --> 00:05:27,445
little as a naming convention nobody followed.
